[07:25:35] Story complete and committed. Summary:
[07:25:35] ## What changed
[07:25:35] **New route pair** on the existing Spring Boot boundary:
[07:25:35] | Request | Result |
[07:25:35] |---|---|
[07:25:35] | `POST /api/sync/account` (no body or `{}`) | **202** `{taskId, operation, statusUrl}` + `Location` — **no outcome field of any kind** |
[07:25:35] | same, with any field / unreadable JSON | **400** `INVALID_REQUEST` / `MALFORMED_REQUEST`, before a task exists |
[07:25:35] | same, while a sync is unfinished | **409** `SYNC_ALREADY_RUNNING`, naming the running task |
[07:25:35] | `GET /api/sync/tasks/{id}` | **200** `PENDING`/`RUNNING`/`SUCCEEDED`/`FAILED` — a failed task is a successful *lookup* |
[07:25:35] | unresolvable id | **404** `TASK_NOT_FOUND` |
[07:25:35] **New reusable facility** `web.task.BackgroundTaskService` (~150 lines, imports only `java.*`): one virtual thread per task; at most one unfinished task per operation key — overlapping triggers are **refused, not queued**; in-memory only, 100 most recent records retained (older terminal evicted, unfinished never); nothing persisted, so a restart loses records and shutdown interrupts work in flight while committed writes stay committed. A later sync/refresh route reuses it and the shared status route by passing its own key.
[07:25:35] `application.AccountRefreshService` is unchanged — the controller hands `refreshAll` to the facility as a method reference, so step order, persistence and first-failure short-circuiting stay inside it. No JavaFX file changed.
[07:25:35] Failure reporting: `SQLException` → the shared `DATA_STORE_UNAVAILABLE`, else `SYNC_FAILED`, message stating completed steps were **not rolled back**; exception text logged server-side only. A second advice (`SyncApiExceptionHandler`) rather than reusing the calculation one, whose messages describe a crafting calculation.
[07:25:35] ## Tests
[07:25:35] `./mvnw test` — **234 tests, 0 failures** (209 pre-existing unchanged + 25 new). The central check uses latches, not sleeps: with `refreshAll()` blocked, the trigger has already returned *and* the advertised status path answers `RUNNING` with no finish time; after release it reaches `SUCCEEDED`. Also exactly-once delegation per accepted task, zero delegations **and no admitted task** per rejected request, the retention bound via 101 submissions, and failure bodies asserted free of completion wording and of leaked host/endpoint/JDBC detail.
[07:25:35] Re-ran, all passing: `Gw2AppSyncAccountIT`, both view auto-refresh ITs, both calculation real-DB equivalence ITs (4 tests — values, not just field names, since the shared entry point gained beans), and `CraftingProfitApiRealDbPerfIT` (max **2344 ms** vs STORY-API-001's 1981 ms — run-to-run variation on a cold first request on a path this story did not modify; still far inside §33's budget, and still backend-only, not proof of the full-page budget). Live non-mutating probes against a real server confirmed the 404/400/400 paths plus framework 404/405 and an unaffected `POST /api/crafting/profit`.
[07:25:35] ## Two things to flag
[07:25:35] - **The accepted path was not run against the live GW2 API** — that would mean a real sync writing to your database, which the story explicitly does not require. Unverified end-to-end is one real `refreshAll()` inside a task; everything around it is proven.
[07:25:35] - **`FAILED` cannot say which steps completed.** The service exposes no step-level progress, and manufacturing one at the boundary would duplicate its orchestration. Recorded as a limitation rather than invented.
[07:25:35] Also recorded `KNOWN_PROBLEMS.md` §7.10: this is the first **write-triggering** route on an API with no authentication (§20 still TBD). Not changed here — that's an undecided architecture question.
[07:25:35] ## Commit
[07:25:35] `ea264f1`, on `master`, not pushed. It carries the whole tree: `src/main/java/web/`, several `application/*` classes and more were untracked from earlier stories, so a story-scoped commit would not have compiled from a clean checkout. The message says so explicitly. `git reset --soft HEAD~1` if you'd rather split it.
[07:25:35] One incidental build fact worth knowing for future routes: this build does not compile with `-parameters`, so `@PathVariable`/`@RequestParam` need their names spelled out (`@PathVariable("taskId")`). The first run failed on it; I spelled the name rather than changing the shared compiler config.
